    dict(key='22-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The appetite, and what is left',
         body="FIVE. (continued)\n  THE CHEAPEST SET WITHIN THE APPETITE:\n    all 7 controls: no smaller set is enough\n    cost $1.7M, $910k over the budget\n  THE DECISION IS THE BOARD'S:\n    spend more, insure the rest, or change the appetite\n  WHAT THE MODEL CANNOT KNOW:\n    the ranges are an expert's guesses, not measurements\n    the risks are independent here; real incidents cluster\n    an incident nobody imagined is not in the register",
         narration='Trying every set for the cheapest one within the appetite gives all seven controls. [[slnc 300]] That is nine hundred and ten thousand dollars over the budget. [[slnc 400]] And what the model cannot know. [[slnc 300]] The ranges are guesses. [[slnc 300]] Real incidents cluster, and here the risks are independent. [[slnc 300]] And an incident nobody imagined is not in the register.'),
    dict(key='23-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=["The inputs are an expert's ranges", 'The risks are taken as independent', 'A number is easy to trust too much', 'Meeting the appetite may cost much more', 'Workshops to agree the ranges take time'],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] The inputs are an expert's ranges, not measurements. [[slnc 500]] The risks are taken as independent, and real incidents often come together. [[slnc 500]] A number with a dollar sign is easy to trust too much. [[slnc 500]] Meeting the appetite may cost much more than the budget: here, all seven controls. [[slnc 300]] And agreeing the ranges takes workshops, and time."),
    dict(key='24-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['A heat map of red, amber and green squares', 'A risk scored 4 for likelihood, 5 for impact', 'A budget spent on the cheapest controls first', 'An appetite statement with no number in it', 'FAIR, ISO 27005, NIST risk guidance'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] A heat map of red, amber, and green squares is a risk register by colour. [[slnc 500]] A risk scored four for likelihood and five for impact hides how much money is at stake. [[slnc 500]] A budget spent on the cheapest controls first is rarely the best buy. [[slnc 500]] An appetite statement with no number in it cannot be checked. [[slnc 500]] And the F A I R method, I S O twenty seven thousand and five, and the N I S T risk guides describe this way of working.'),
    dict(key='25-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Write each risk as two ranges', 'Simulate, and look at the tail', 'Compare controls on the same years', 'Choose by net benefit, not by price', 'State the appetite as a number', 'Say what the model assumes'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Write each risk as two ranges: how often, and how much. [[slnc 500]] Simulate many years, and look at the tail, the bad year, not just the average. [[slnc 500]] Compare controls on the same simulated years, and choose by net benefit, not by price. [[slnc 500]] State the appetite as a number, and test against it. [[slnc 300]] And say plainly what the model assumes.'),
    dict(key='26-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Tri: a range: least, most likely, most', 'Simulation: 50,000 years, the same years for every treatment', 'YearSteps: a teaching class: one year redone by hand', 'Optimizer: the best set of controls within a budget'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] It needs only the J D K. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter.'),
    dict(key='27-class-1', kind='code', title='Tri — a draw between 0 and 1 becomes a value',
         body='// Tri: a range: least, most likely, most\npublic double at(double u) {\n    double split = (max - min) == 0 ? 0 : (mode - min) / (max - min);\n    if (u < split) {\n        return min + Math.sqrt(u * (max - min) * (mode - min));\n    }\n    return max - Math.sqrt((1 - u) * (max - min) * (max - mode));',
         narration="A draw between zero and one is a share of the triangle's area. [[slnc 300]] Below the most likely point, the value is read from the least end; above it, from the most end. [[slnc 300]] This is the formula the first act worked out by hand."),
    dict(key='28-class-2', kind='code', title='Simulation — the draws are made once and kept',
         body='// Simulation: 50,000 years, the same years for every treatment\nRandomGenerator rng = RandomGeneratorFactory.of(ALGORITHM).create(seed + 7919L * r);\nfor (int t = 0; t < trials; t++) {\n    frequencyU[r][t] = rng.nextDouble();\n    eventsU[r][t] = rng.nextDouble();',
         narration='Each risk gets its own seeded random generator, of a fixed algorithm. [[slnc 300]] The draws are made once and kept. [[slnc 300]] So two sets of controls are compared on exactly the same years, and the difference is the controls, not the luck.'),
    dict(key='29-class-3', kind='code', title='Simulation — how many events: walk up the chances',
         body='// Simulation: 50,000 years, the same years for every treatment\ndouble p = StrictMath.exp(-lambda);\ndouble cumulative = p;\nint k = 0;\nwhile (u > cumulative && k < MAX_EVENTS) {\n    k++;\n    p *= lambda / k;\n    cumulative += p;',
         narration='The chance of no event is e to the minus rate. [[slnc 300]] The loop adds the chance of one, two, three events, until the draw is reached. [[slnc 300]] Strict math keeps the result the same on every machine.'),
    dict(key='30-class-4', kind='code', title='Simulation — a control multiplies the rate and the loss',
         body='// Simulation: 50,000 years, the same years for every treatment\ndouble lambda = risk.frequency().at(frequencyU[riskIndex][t]) * fFactor;\nint events = poisson(lambda, eventsU[riskIndex][t]);\n...\nsum += risk.loss().at(lossU[riskIndex][t][j]) * lFactor;',
         narration="One year of one risk: the rate, then the number of events, then a loss for each event. [[slnc 300]] A control's factors multiply the rate and the loss."),
    dict(key='31-class-5', kind='code', title='YearSteps — the same draws, step by step',
         body='// YearSteps: a teaching class: one year redone by hand\ndouble u1 = sim.frequencyDraw(riskIndex, year);\ndouble split = (f.mode() - f.min()) / (f.max() - f.min());\ndouble rate = f.at(u1);\n...\nwhile (u2 > cumulative && events < Simulation.MAX_EVENTS) {',
         narration="This class redoes one simulated year, keeping every step: the draws, the split, the rate, and the chances. [[slnc 300]] A test proves that its total is exactly the simulation's loss, for every risk and every year."),
    dict(key='32-class-6', kind='code', title='Optimizer — try every set, keep the largest net benefit',
         body='// Optimizer: the best set of controls within a budget\nfor (int mask = 1; mask < (1 << available.size()); mask++) {\n...\n    if (cost > budget) {\n        continue;\n    }\n    double mean = Simulation.mean(sim.portfolio(set));\n    double net = baseline - mean - cost;',
         narration='Each bit of the mask says whether a control is in the set: one hundred and twenty seven sets for seven controls. [[slnc 300]] A set over the budget is skipped. [[slnc 300]] The net benefit is the loss before, minus the loss after, minus the cost.'),
